INT. TRAIN STATION LEFT-LUGGAGE OFFICE - NIGHT

Rows of steel shelves. Suitcases, cello cases, a taxidermy owl in a crate. A clock above the counter reads 11:52.

CORDELIA MBEKI, thirties, stands at the counter with a claim ticket. She slides it across. Behind the counter, AUGUSTINE PELLETIER, an older clerk, takes it without looking and shuffles away into the shelves.

She waits. Then the door behind her opens and RAFAELA DOMINGUES walks in, shaking rain off her coat. She stops when she sees her.

She doesn't move. Cordelia turns her head and stops as well. The rain on the roof is the only sound.

RAFAELA
You came.

CORDELIA
I said I would.

She steps to the counter and sets down a small leather case. She doesn't open it. Cordelia looks at the case, then at her.

CORDELIA (CONT'D)
That's the one?

RAFAELA
That's the one.

She pushes it an inch toward her. Cordelia puts a finger on the lid and takes it away again.

CORDELIA
I thought you'd have thrown it into the river by now.

RAFAELA
I thought about it.

CORDELIA
What stopped you?

RAFAELA
The river was cold.

Augustine returns with a battered suitcase. He sets it on the counter and looks from one to the other. She lifts her eyes to meet his.

AUGUSTINE
Ticket three-oh-seven.

CORDELIA
That's mine.

She grips the handle. Rafaela lays a hand on the other end of it. They stand with the suitcase between them, both holding on.

RAFAELA
Your train is at midnight.

CORDELIA
It is.

RAFAELA
Leave it.

CORDELIA
I can't.

Rafaela lets go. She picks up her leather case and holds it against her chest. Cordelia drags the suitcase off the counter, and at the door she pauses with her back turned.

CORDELIA (CONT'D)
Keep the case. Please.

She goes out into the rain. Rafaela stays where she is until the clock reads 11:58, then lays the case on the counter in front of the clerk.

RAFAELA
Could you hold this for me?

Augustine takes it and writes out a ticket.
